WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.200
Latvia holds a parliamentary election today as voters weigh national security against rising living costs in the NATO member that borders Russia. This is Omni View, episode 194. Around 1.5 million people were eligible to vote in Latvia's 11th parliamentary election since independence from the Soviet Union. The vote filled 100 seats in the Siamma through a proportional system contested by 14 parties. Current Prime Minister Andres Kulbergz,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.280
in office since May, led a center-right United List alliance that topped polls.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.220
The coalition was expected to emerge weakened as populist parties gained ground.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.340
The election occurred against Russia's ongoing invasion of Ukraine,

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:49.080
viewed in Latvia as a direct security threat given its own history as a former Soviet state

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:56.140
with a sizable ethnic Russian minority. Latvia has committed by law to spending at least 5% of GDP

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.480
IP on defense, which leaves less room for other spending.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:06.200
Many Latvians report high prices, low wages, a sluggish economy, strained health services,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.040
and persistent emigration alongside an aging population.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.120
Populist groups such as the sovereign power, young Latvians alliance, and the Latvia first

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:22.080
party have sought to tap discontent by advocating a softer line toward Moscow and questioning

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.980
the costs of full economic decoupling from Russia.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:25.080
Russia.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.080
No major party has proposed shifting Latvia's alignment with the EU and NATO.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.980
Four German Eurofighter jets were deployed over Latvian airspace during the poll at Riga's

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:36.440
request.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.880
Partial results were expected Saturday evening, with official preliminary results due Sunday,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.820
after which coalition talks were likely to begin.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.620
The Baltic state's location next to both Russia and Belarus has kept defense spending high,

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:55.560
even as households face everyday pressures from inflation and limited wage growth.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:00.860
Voters must choose among parties that all maintain the country's EU and NATO commitments

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.060
while differing on how quickly to ease domestic costs.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:09.500
The proportional allocation of the 100 seats means the leading alliance will need partners

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:14.360
to reach a governing majority, a process that often stretches beyond the initial count.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.840
Doit Chavella reports that the security context remains central for many voters given the

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:20.720
border with Russia.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.320
Regional coverage also notes that domestic economic strains have lifted support for parties

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.360
offering quicker relief on costs.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.340
Even supporters of the leading alliance acknowledge that forming a stable coalition will require

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.200
concessions on spending priorities.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:42.100
What happens next centers on coalition negotiations that begin after official results.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:48.920
